{
  "plan": {
    "overview": "Creating a paper airplane that flies far requires a combination of design, folding, and throwing techniques. This plan will guide you through the process of making a basic paper airplane that can fly a significant distance.",
    "rationale": "A well-designed paper airplane with the right balance of weight, lift, and thrust can achieve impressive distances. By following this plan, you'll learn how to create a plane with a stable flight path and sufficient aerodynamics to soar through the air."
  },
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Step 1: Choose the Right Paper",
      "detail": "Select a square piece of paper with a minimum size of 8.5 x 11 inches (21.5 x 28 cm). You can use any type of paper, but heavier paper (such as construction paper or cardstock) will generally produce better results."
    },
    {
      "title": "Step 2: Fold the Paper in Half",
      "detail": "Fold the paper in half diagonally to create a crease in the middle. This will help you find the center of the paper and create a symmetrical design."
    },
    {
      "title": "Step 3: Create the Wing Folds",
      "detail": "Open the paper and fold the top left and right corners down so that they meet the middle crease. The folds should be symmetrical and about 1 inch (2.5 cm) deep."
    },
    {
      "title": "Step 4: Create the Tail Fold",
      "detail": "Fold the bottom corner of the paper up so that it meets the middle crease. This fold should be about 0.5 inches (1.3 cm) deep."
    },
    {
      "title": "Step 5: Assemble the Plane",
      "detail": "Your paper airplane should now have a distinctive shape with two wings and a tail. Gently pull the wings and tail to ensure they are even and symmetrical. Make any necessary adjustments to the folds to achieve a smooth, aerodynamic shape."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "By following these steps, you should be able to create a basic paper airplane that can fly a significant distance. To throw the plane, hold it by the middle crease and gently toss it forward with a smooth, underhand motion. Aim for a slight upward trajectory to give the plane enough lift to soar through the air. With practice, you can refine your design and throwing technique to achieve even greater distances."
}
